A = {
60: """There are so many popular dishes in Vietnam, but I'd like to talk about pho, which is probably our most famous dish. Pho is a noodle soup made with flat rice noodles and a clear broth, usually served with thin slices of beef or chicken. What makes it special is the broth. It's cooked for many hours with beef bones and spices like star anise, cinnamon, and grilled ginger and onion, so it has a really rich and fragrant taste. When you eat pho, you can add fresh herbs, bean sprouts, lime, and chili to make it taste the way you like. People in Vietnam usually eat pho for breakfast, but you can find it at any time of the day. It's cheap, healthy, and warm, so it's perfect on a cold morning. For me, a bowl of pho always tastes like home.""",
61: """On a regular day, I usually eat three meals. For breakfast, I don't have much time, so I often grab a banh mi, which is a Vietnamese sandwich with eggs or pork, or sometimes a bowl of noodle soup near my house. I also drink a cup of iced coffee to wake up. For lunch, I eat at the company canteen. The meals there are simple but balanced, usually rice with some meat or fish, stir-fried vegetables, and a bowl of soup, and we often have fruit like bananas or watermelon for dessert. Dinner is the most important meal, because it's the only time my whole family eats together. My wife usually cooks rice with two or three dishes, like braised pork, fried fish, and boiled vegetables. Sometimes, if I'm hungry at night, I have a small snack, like yogurt or some fruit.""",
62: """I remember a bad experience with food during a business trip to Ho Chi Minh City about two years ago. On the last night, my coworkers took me to a famous street food area to try some local dishes. We ate a lot of things, like grilled snails, raw oysters, and spicy seafood. Everything tasted amazing at the time. But around midnight, I woke up with a terrible stomachache, and I kept running to the bathroom. I think the oysters weren't fresh. The next morning, I felt so weak that I almost couldn't get on my flight. My coworker bought me some medicine and an electrolyte drink from a pharmacy, and I slept for the whole flight. It was memorable because I learned a hard lesson. Since then, I've been much more careful about eating raw seafood, especially at street stalls in hot weather.""",
63: """The last time I had some really healthy food was last Sunday. My wife and I decided to cook a healthy lunch at home with our daughter, because we had been eating a lot of fast food that week. In the morning, we went to the market together and bought fresh vegetables, some salmon, and fruit. My daughter helped wash the vegetables, which she thought was really fun. We made a big salad with lettuce, tomatoes, cucumbers, and boiled eggs, and we grilled the salmon with a little lemon and garlic. For dessert, we had a plate of mango and watermelon. Everything tasted fresh and light, and I felt much better after the meal, not heavy and sleepy like after eating fried food. What was special was that we cooked it together as a family, so even my daughter ate all her vegetables without complaining.""",
64: """When I was a child, people's ideas about health were quite simple. Most people thought that being healthy meant eating enough and being a little chubby, because food wasn't always easy to get. A healthy diet was mostly rice, vegetables from the garden, and some fish or pork. People rarely ate fast food or sugary snacks, mostly because they couldn't afford them. As for exercise, very few people went to the gym. Most adults did physical work every day, like farming or cycling to work, and kids just played outside. Older people did some exercise in the park in the early morning. These days, things have changed a lot. People are much more aware of nutrition, calories, and sugar. Many people go to the gym, do yoga, or count their steps every day. People also talk more about mental health and sleep, not just physical health.""",
65: """I'd like to talk about my uncle, who is one of the healthiest people I know. He's almost sixty, but he looks much younger than his age, and he's always full of energy. What makes him so healthy is his routine. Every morning, he gets up at five and goes jogging around the lake near his house for about forty minutes. After that, he does some stretching and simple exercises in the park with his friends. He also eats very carefully. He eats lots of vegetables and fish, and he avoids fried food, sweets, and alcohol, even at family parties, which surprises everyone. Another thing is that he always goes to bed early and never stays up late. He's also a very calm and positive person, and he rarely gets stressed. I really admire him, and he motivates me to take better care of my health.""",
66: """Yes, about two years ago I changed some of my habits to improve my health. Back then, I used to stay up late watching videos on my phone, and I drank soft drinks almost every day. I always felt tired, and I gained a few kilos. Then, during my annual health check at work, the doctor said my blood sugar was a bit high, which really worried me. So I decided to make some changes. First, I set a rule for myself: no phone after eleven at night. Then I replaced soft drinks with water and unsweetened tea, which was the hardest part, because I really missed the taste. A few weeks later, I started jogging three mornings a week. After about two months, I noticed a big difference. I slept better, had more energy at work, and lost about four kilos.""",
67: """As I mentioned, one of the main things I did for my health was to start jogging regularly. I go jogging three mornings a week, for about thirty minutes, around the lake in the park near my house. At first, it was really difficult. I got tired after just ten minutes, and my legs hurt for days. But I kept going slowly, and after about a month, it became much easier. The impact on my health has been really positive. Physically, I've lost some weight, and my blood sugar is now normal, according to my last health check. I also feel stronger and don't get out of breath when I climb stairs. Mentally, jogging helps me reduce stress, because it gives me time to clear my head before work. I also sleep much better at night. So jogging has really changed my life in a good way.""",
68: """Vietnam is a long, narrow country shaped like the letter S, and it has really diverse geography. In the north, there are lots of mountains, especially in provinces like Ha Giang, Lao Cai, and Lai Chau. The highest mountain, Fansipan, is near Sa Pa, and it's called the roof of Indochina. In the middle of the country, there's a long mountain range on the west and a very long coastline on the east, so central Vietnam has many beautiful beaches, like in Da Nang and Nha Trang. There are also two huge river deltas. The Red River Delta is in the north, around Hanoi, and the Mekong Delta is in the south. These areas are flat and full of rice fields. Because of this geography, Vietnam has many different landscapes, from mountains and rice terraces to beaches and islands, which makes it great for traveling.""",
69: """My favorite place from my childhood is a small river near my grandparents' village. Every summer, my parents sent me there for a few weeks. The river wasn't very wide, and the water was clear and quite shallow in some places. On both sides, there were green rice fields and big bamboo trees that made a lot of shade. In the afternoons, my cousins and I used to go there to swim, catch small fish, and fly kites on the riverbank. In the evenings, the buffaloes came down to the river to drink water, and the farmers walked home along the small path. I remember the sound of frogs and the cool breeze at sunset. It was a very simple and peaceful place. Sadly, a lot has changed now, because there's a road and some factories nearby, but I still remember it as a perfect place.""",
70: """I had a memorable experience when I climbed Fansipan mountain with two friends about four years ago. Fansipan is the highest mountain in Vietnam, near Sa Pa. These days, most people take the cable car to the top, but we decided to trek up on foot. We started very early in the morning with a local guide. The first few hours were fine, but then the path became very steep and slippery, and it started raining. We were exhausted and our legs were shaking. At night, we stayed in a small camp halfway up the mountain, and it was freezing cold. The next morning, we finally reached the top. At first, we could only see clouds, but after a few minutes, the clouds moved away, and we could see the mountains and valleys below us. It was breathtaking, and I felt really proud of myself.""",
71: """One of my earliest memories of my country's geography is my first trip to Ha Long Bay when I was about eight years old. My parents took me there with my uncle's family. I remember standing on the boat and seeing hundreds of huge limestone islands rising out of the green water. They looked like giant animals or faces, and my father told me the legend about dragons coming down to protect Vietnam, which is where the name Ha Long comes from. That story really amazed me. We also visited a big cave on one of the islands. Inside, it was dark and cool, and there were colorful lights on the rocks, so it felt like a magical world. That trip made me realize how beautiful my country is. Even now, whenever I see a picture of Ha Long Bay, I remember that boat trip with my family.""",
72: """I have quite a lot of furniture in my apartment, but most of it is simple and practical. In the living room, there's a gray sofa, a small wooden coffee table, and a TV stand with some shelves. In the kitchen, we have a dining table with four chairs. In my bedroom, there's a big bed, a wardrobe, and a small desk by the window. My daughter's room has a little bed, a toy box, and a small table where she draws. My favorite piece of furniture is the sofa in the living room. We bought it about two years ago, and it's really soft and comfortable. It's also big enough for the whole family to sit together. After a long day at work, I love lying on it, watching TV, or just taking a short nap. My daughter also likes jumping on it, which my wife doesn't like very much.""",
73: """I use my furniture all day long, from morning to night. In the morning, I wake up in my bed, of course, and then I take my clothes out of the wardrobe and get dressed. I have a quick breakfast at the dining table with my family before going to work. After work, the sofa becomes the center of my evening. I usually sit there to watch the news, play with my daughter, or just relax. Sometimes I fall asleep there by accident. After dinner, if I need to finish some work or study English, I sit at the desk in my bedroom with my laptop. My daughter also uses the coffee table in the living room to draw and do her puzzles. Before bed, I read a few pages of a book in bed. So I'd say the sofa and my bed are the pieces of furniture I use the most.""",
74: """When I was a child, our house didn't have much furniture, and most of it was made of heavy dark wood. In the living room, there was a big wooden table with a few wooden chairs and a long wooden bench, which were really hard and not very comfortable. There was also a big wooden cabinet where my parents kept a small TV and their important things. In the bedroom, we had a wooden bed with a thin mattress and a mosquito net. Today, the furniture I use is very different. First, it's more comfortable, like my soft sofa and my thick mattress. Second, it's lighter and more modern, made of different materials like plastic, metal, and fabric. Also, the old furniture was built to last for decades, but modern furniture is cheaper and people replace it more often. I sometimes miss that old wooden table, though.""",
75: """I had a problem with my furniture about a year ago. We bought a new wardrobe for our bedroom from a furniture store, and it was delivered as separate pieces, so we had to assemble it ourselves. I followed the instructions carefully, and it took me almost the whole Saturday afternoon. Everything looked fine, but about a week later, when my wife opened the door, the door suddenly came off and almost fell on her. Luckily, she wasn't hurt. When I checked, I found that one of the screws holding the door was broken. I called the store and explained the problem. At first, they said I probably assembled it wrong, but I sent them some photos, and they agreed it was a factory defect. A technician came two days later and replaced the broken part for free. Since then, I always check everything carefully after I assemble furniture.""",
76: """People in my country do almost everything on the internet these days. Young people spend a lot of time on social media, like Facebook and TikTok, where they watch short videos, share photos, and chat with friends. Many people also watch movies and TV series on streaming apps, or watch videos on YouTube. Online shopping has become really popular too. People buy clothes, cosmetics, and even groceries on shopping apps, because it's cheap and convenient. Besides that, people use the internet for practical things, like online banking, paying bills, booking taxis, and ordering food. Students often take online classes and search for information for their homework. And of course, many people, especially teenagers, play online games for hours. I think the internet has become such a big part of daily life that most people can't imagine living without it.""",
77: """I use the internet every day for many different things. In the morning, I usually read the news on my phone while having breakfast. At work, I use the internet mostly for emails and online meetings. In the evening, I relax by watching videos on YouTube, especially cooking channels and videos about technology. I also like learning English online. I use a few apps and watch some English videos to practice my listening. As for shopping, yes, I shop online quite often, maybe two or three times a month. I usually buy things like clothes, household items, and toys for my daughter, because it's cheaper and they deliver to my door. I don't really share videos with other people, but I sometimes post photos of my family trips on Facebook so my relatives can see them. I also use the internet for online banking and paying bills.""",
78: """My first experience with the internet was when I was in middle school, around thirteen years old. At that time, my family didn't have a computer, so my friends and I used to go to an internet cafe near our school. It was a small, dark room with about twenty old computers, and it was always full of boys playing online games and smoking, which my mother didn't like at all. I remember the internet was really slow. It took several minutes just to open a website, and sometimes the connection was lost completely. The first thing I did online was create an email account, and then I used Yahoo Messenger to chat with my classmates. I was so excited when I sent my first message. Compared to now, it was really basic, but at that time, it felt like a magical new world.""",
79: """A memorable posting I saw on the internet was a video that went viral in Vietnam a couple of years ago. It showed a young delivery driver in Hanoi who found a wallet full of money and documents on the street. Instead of keeping it, he spent the whole afternoon trying to find the owner, even though it meant he couldn't make deliveries and lost money that day. In the end, he found the owner, an old woman who had just withdrawn money to pay for her husband's hospital bill. She cried and tried to give him a reward, but he refused. The video was shared thousands of times, and many people left comments praising him. It was memorable for me because there's a lot of negative news online, and this story reminded me that there are still many kind and honest people around us.""",
80: """What I like most about my phone is the camera. I have a daughter, so I take photos and videos of her almost every day, and the camera on my phone is really good. The pictures are sharp and colorful, even at night or indoors, and it has a portrait mode that makes the background blurry, so the photos look professional. I also like the large screen, because it's great for watching videos and reading. Besides that, there are a few apps I use all the time. My banking app is really useful, because I can transfer money or pay bills in a few seconds. I also like the map app, because it helps me find places easily when I travel. And of course, the battery lasts for the whole day, so I don't need to worry about charging it all the time.""",
81: """Besides talking to people, I use my phone for lots of things every day. In the morning, I check the weather and read news headlines. During the day, I use messaging apps a lot, because my team at work shares updates in a group chat. I also use my phone for payments. Almost every shop accepts QR codes now, so I rarely carry cash anymore. In the evening, I usually watch short videos or cooking videos on YouTube to relax. As for social media, I'm not very active. I only post photos on Facebook a few times a month, usually from family trips or special occasions. I don't play many games, but sometimes I play a simple puzzle game when I'm waiting for something. On weekends, I also use apps to order food, book taxis, and make video calls with my parents.""",
82: """These days, I don't make as many phone calls as I used to, because most of my friends prefer texting. But I still call my close friends maybe once or twice a week. When we talk, we usually start by asking about each other's families and work. Since most of us have small kids now, we often talk about our children, like their schools or funny things they did. We also talk about our plans, for example, when we can meet up for coffee or a weekend trip. Sometimes, if there's an important football match, we call each other to talk about it before and after the game. With my best friend, the calls are usually longer, because we talk about more serious things, like money, career plans, or problems at work. Talking on the phone helps us stay close, even though we're all busy.""",
83: """I bought my current phone about a year ago, after my old phone started having battery problems. Before buying it, I did quite a lot of research. I read online reviews and watched comparison videos on YouTube. I also asked a coworker who knows a lot about technology, and he recommended this model. There were a few things that made me choose it. First, the camera was really important to me, because I take a lot of photos of my daughter, and this phone has one of the best cameras in its price range. Second, the battery life was great, so I don't need to charge it during the day. Third, the price was reasonable, and there was a discount at the store when I bought it. Finally, I'd used the same brand before, so I was used to the system and it was easy to switch.""",
84: """In Vietnam, the most common form of technology is definitely the smartphone. Almost everyone has one, from teenagers to older people, even in the countryside. People use their phones for everything, like chatting, social media, banking, and shopping. Computers are also common, especially laptops. Students use them for studying, and office workers use them every day for their jobs. In many homes, there are also smart TVs, which people use to watch movies and YouTube. In recent years, some other kinds of technology have become more popular, like smartwatches, wireless earphones, and robot vacuum cleaners. Another big change is digital payment. People now pay with QR codes or banking apps almost everywhere, even at small street food stalls. So I'd say technology has really become a part of daily life in my country.""",
85: """The piece of technology I use most often is definitely my smartphone. I use it from the moment I wake up until I go to bed. My alarm is on my phone, so it's literally the first thing I touch in the morning. While I'm having breakfast, I check the weather and read the news. During the day, I use it mainly for messaging, because my team shares updates in a group chat at work. I also use it for payments, because I pay for almost everything with QR codes now. In the evening, I relax by watching videos and reading. I also use a laptop at work for emails and reports, but the phone is more important in my daily life. To be honest, I think I depend on it a bit too much, so recently I've started leaving it outside the bedroom at night.""",
86: """One of my early memories about technology is my family's first mobile phone. It was a small Nokia phone that my father bought when I was about ten years old. It had a tiny black-and-white screen and real buttons, and you could only make calls and send short text messages. I remember the only game on it was Snake, and my sister and I fought over who could play it. My father was very proud of that phone, and he kept it in a leather case. Compared to that, today's phones are completely different. They have huge touch screens, great cameras, and internet access, and you can do almost anything with them, like banking, shopping, and watching movies. The old phone was very simple, but its battery lasted almost a week, which is something I really miss about it.""",
87: """I had a problem with technology about six months ago, and it was really stressful. I was preparing an important presentation for a meeting with my manager. I worked on it for two days on my laptop. The night before the meeting, my laptop suddenly froze and then shut down completely. When I restarted it, I realized I hadn't saved the file for over an hour, and part of my work was gone. I panicked, because it was already eleven o'clock at night. First, I tried to find an automatic backup, but I could only recover an older version. So I had to stay up until almost two in the morning to redo the missing slides. Luckily, the presentation went well the next day. Since then, I always save my work to a cloud drive, and I've turned on automatic saving.""",
88: """When I go shopping, I usually go to a big supermarket in a shopping mall near my house. I go there about once a week, usually on Saturday morning with my wife and daughter. We mostly buy groceries for the week, like rice, meat, fish, vegetables, milk, and snacks, and also household items like dish soap and toilet paper. Sometimes we also buy clothes or toys in other shops in the mall. What's special about this place is that it has everything in one building, so it's very convenient. The supermarket is clean and air-conditioned, and the prices are clearly marked, so I don't need to bargain like at the traditional market. There's also a food court and a playground on the top floor, so after shopping, we usually have lunch there and my daughter can play for a while. It's like a small family outing every weekend.""",
89: """One of my early memories of shopping is the traditional market in my hometown. When I was a kid, my mother often took me there on Sunday mornings. The market was in an old building with a big roof, and around it there were lots of small stalls outside. Inside, it was crowded, noisy, and a bit muddy, especially on rainy days. People sold everything there, like fresh vegetables, live chickens, fish in big buckets, clothes, and plastic toys. I remember the smell of fresh herbs mixed with the smell of fish. My mother always bargained with the sellers, which I found really interesting. My favorite part was the snack section, because if I behaved well, she would buy me a small cake or sweet soup. My impression was that it was a lively and exciting place, full of colors and sounds.""",
}
